# Vox — Hyprland hotkey bindings
#
# Hyprland never records audio itself. The daemon (systemd user service
# voxlinux.service) owns the microphone and listens on a control socket at
# $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/voxlinux.sock; the binds just send it `start` / `stop`.
#
# The ready-to-use fragment for this machine (Hyprland 0.56, Lua config,
# Omarchy) is hyprland-bind.lua — copy it to ~/.config/hypr/ and
# `require("vox-bindings")` it from ~/.config/hypr/bindings.lua.
#
# Everything below is reference for other setups.
#
#   1. NO CONFIG AT ALL (only with /dev/input access). Vox watches KEY_RCTRL
#      on the keyboard itself and needs no binds:
#          sudo usermod -aG input $USER      # then re-login
#
#   2. BINDS (fallback, and what hyprland-bind.lua uses). Below.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Push-to-talk needs press *and* release
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A plain bind fires on press. Adding the `R` flag fires it on release
# instead, so the two together behave like a real push-to-talk:
#
#     held      → start recording
#     released  → stop, transcribe, insert at the cursor
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Option A — Lua config (Hyprland 0.56+, Omarchy)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# In ~/.config/hypr/bindings.lua. Verified against `o.bind` on this setup:
#
#     o.bind("Right",             "Vox: start recording", "vox-ptt start")
#     o.bind("Right",             "Vox: stop",           "vox-ptt stop",
#                                { release = true })
#     hl.unbind("SUPER + SHIFT + D")
#     o.bind("SUPER + SHIFT + D", "Vox: toggle",         "vox-ptt toggle")
#
# Two key-string rules learned on 0.56: the key is an X keysym — `Right` is
# right Ctrl (`RCTRL`/`RCTRC` are not keysyms and are rejected) — and every
# modifier needs its own "+": "SUPER SHIFT + D" fails as an unknown keysym.
#
# If `o.bind` cannot express the release direction for your key, use the
# `hold` command instead: it starts when the key is down and stops by itself
# once Hyprland reports the key released.
#
#     o.bind("Right", "Vox: push-to-talk", "vox-ptt hold Right")
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Option B — legacy .conf syntax
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# For Hyprland builds that still parse `bind =` lines. Note that recent
# Hyprland (0.56) refuses `bind` lines when running the Lua config parser, so
# check whether your setup accepts them before relying on this block.
#
# Press and release:
#
#     bind  = Right, exec, vox-ptt start
#     bind  = Right, R,   exec, vox-ptt stop
#
# Locked variant — grabs the key while it is held, which keeps the hotkey
# from also firing alongside your own CTRL shortcuts:
#
#     bindl = Right, exec, vox-ptt start
#     bindl = Right, R,   exec, vox-ptt stop
#
# A non-modifier key sidesteps the CTRL conflict entirely; Pause/Break makes a
# good push-to-talk on most keyboards:
#
#     bind = Pause, exec, vox-ptt start
#     bind = Pause, R,   exec, vox-ptt stop
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Always-safe alternative: toggle
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Bind a combo you do not use on its own. Each press toggles recording, so
# no press/release tracking is needed at all:
#
#     bind = SUPER SHIFT D, exec, vox-ptt toggle
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Commands
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# vox-ptt (scripts/vox-ptt) forwards one command to the worker:
#
#     start        begin recording
#     stop         end recording → transcribe → insert
#     toggle       recording ? stop : start
#     hold <KEY>   start, then stop automatically when KEY is released
#     ping         check that the worker is alive
#
# `hold` polls the compositor (`hyprctl repl` + hl.is_key_down, ~8 ms per
# poll). It only tracks real keyboard input: keys injected through the
# virtual-keyboard protocol (wtype) do not register, so `hold` stops
# immediately under wtype. The press/release bind pair above is preferred.
#
# By hand, without vox-ptt:
#
#     printf 'start' | socat - UNIX-CONNECT:"$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/voxlinux.sock"
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Requirements
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# * wtype inserts the recognised text at the cursor (virtual-keyboard
#   protocol). Without it Vox can transcribe but not paste:
#       sudo pacman -S wtype
# * The systemd user service must be running; check it with
#       systemctl --user status voxlinux